<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · 1847 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Abide with Me</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A hymn asking God to stay close in life and in death, written in 1847 by a dying English vicar and sung before every FA Cup Final.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Henry Francis Lyte</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>William Henry Monk (the tune &quot;Eventide&quot;, 1861); Lyte wrote an air of his own for it</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>British Anglicans</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>England</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>1847 (an account of 1925 dates it to 1820)</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (words by Henry Francis Lyte (d. 1847))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abide-with-me">Abide with Me</a>&quot; is a Christian hymn by the Anglican cleric Henry Francis Lyte (1793–1847). Lyte was a curate in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wexford">County Wexford</a> from 1815 to 1818, where he often preached at the church in Killurin and came under the lasting influence of its rector, the Reverend Abraham Swanne; he was later vicar of All Saints' Church in Brixham, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devon">Devon</a>. In poor health for most of his life, he travelled abroad for relief, as was usual at the time. The hymn takes its starting point from Luke 24:29, where the disciples ask <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> to abide with them &quot;for it is toward evening and the day is far spent&quot;.</p><p>The date of the text is disputed. An article in The Spectator of 3 October 1925 said that Lyte wrote it in 1820, while staying with the Hore family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-wexford">County Wexford</a>, after sitting with a dying friend, William Augustus Le <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hunte">Hunte</a>, who kept repeating the words &quot;abide with me&quot;; on this account Lyte recalled those lines twenty-seven years later, when he was dying of tuberculosis. The usual date is 1847. His daughter, Anna Maria Maxwell Hogg, told how in September of that year, against his family's fears, he preached once more to his people, and that evening gave a near relative the little hymn with an air of his own composing. Lyte died weeks later, on 20 November 1847, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, then in the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>, and the hymn was first sung at his funeral.</p><p>The tune most often sung is not Lyte's but &quot;Eventide&quot;, written in 1861 by the English organist William Henry Monk, the first editor of Hymns Ancient and Modern; one edition of that hymnal left out a verse about the singer's youth as too personal. Other tunes followed, among them Samuel Liddle's setting of 1896, the one Dame Clara Butt favoured. Ralph Vaughan Williams wrote a descant for the hymnal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Praise (1925) and an orchestral prelude on Eventide for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hereford">Hereford</a> Festival of 1936, and Charles Ives set the hymn around 1890.</p><p>The hymn is sung in many Christian denominations, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-v">George V</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahatma-gandhi">Mahatma Gandhi</a> were said to hold it a favourite. It is often sung at funerals: those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sun-yat-sen">Sun Yat-sen</a> in 1925, of George V in 1936, of Queen Mary in 1953, of Jean, Grand Duke of Luxembourg, in 2019 and of Harald V of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 2026. It was sung at the burial of the Unknown Warrior in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> in 1920, and is sometimes sung on Anzac Day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and on Remembrance Day in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the United Kingdom.</p><p>Its first and last verses are sung at the FA Cup Final about 15 minutes before kick-off. The custom may go back to the 1923 final between Bolton Wanderers and West Ham United, when the event choir sang it while crowds were cleared from the pitch; its first formal use was at the 1927 final between Arsenal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardiff">Cardiff</a> City, and it has been sung before every Rugby League Challenge Cup Final since 1929. Emeli Sandé sang it at the opening ceremony of the 2012 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> Olympics, in tribute to the victims of the 7/7 terrorist attacks. Thelonious Monk recorded an arrangement in 1957, and in 2019 the programme <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Songs</a> of Praise named it the fifth most popular hymn among British Christians.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A prayer that God stay with the one who prays as evening falls, through the trials of life and at the hour of death, taking its first words from the disciples' plea to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in Luke 24:29. Near its close it takes up the challenge to death and the grave of 1 Corinthians 15:55. The mood is quiet, solemn and trusting.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Abide with me! fast falls the eventide;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The darkness deepens; Lord, with me abide!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>When other helpers fail,and comforts flee,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Help of the helpless, O abide with me!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Swift to its close ebbs out life's little day;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Earth's joys grow dim, its glories pass away;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Change and decay in all around I see;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O thou who changest not, abide with me!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I need thy presence every passing hour;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>What but thy grace can foil the tempter's power?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Who like thyself my guide and stay can be?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Through cloud and sunshine, O abide with me!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I fear no foe, with thee at hand to bless;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Ills have no weight, and tears no bitterness:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where is death's sting? where, grave thy victory?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>I triumph still if thou abide with me!</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Hold thou thy cross before my closing eyes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>shine through the gloom, and point me to the skies:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Heaven's morning breaks, and earth's vain shadows flee;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>In life and death, O Lord abide with me!</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>A-men</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Henry Francis Lyte (1847), as in The Army and Navy Hymnal (1920). Text from <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/The_Army_and_Navy_Hymnal%2FHymns%2FAbide_with_Me!_Fast_Falls_the_Eventide" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="D3WSX5LTFt8" data-title="Abide with Me: the Choir of King's College, Cambridge, under Stephen Cleobury, sing the hymn to Monk's Eventide (Hymns from King's)"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">the Choir of King's College, Cambridge, under Stephen Cleobury, sing the hymn to Monk's Eventide (Hymns from King's)</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel The Choir of King’s College, Cambridge - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D3WSX5LTFt8" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Eventide (William Henry Monk)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1861</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune most often sung to it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Symphony No. 9, fourth movement (Gustav Mahler)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">principal theme often noted as like Eventide</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Two Hymn-Tune Preludes: Eventide (Ralph Vaughan Williams)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1936</span><span class="sg-chain-h">orchestral prelude on the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Abide with Me (Charles Ives)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1890</span><span class="sg-chain-h">song setting, published 1958</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Abide with Me (Thelonious Monk)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1957</span><span class="sg-chain-h">jazz arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Burmese Days (George Orwell)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">referred to in a novel</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Luke 24:29</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the opening line alludes to the verse</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">1 Corinthians 15:55</span><span class="sg-chain-h">drawn on in the penultimate verse</span></li></ul></section>
